feov-migrate reads an old run's record — including a vocabulary the record tool's readers refuse — translates each event through the authored registry, and replays them in order through the current write path under the ORIGINAL clock. Every act is validated exactly as a seat's would be, so the output is a record feov-record of the same release could have written; run channels (inputs, proofs) copy verbatim, and inputs/migration.json records what was translated, refused, and accepted as loss. A refusal exits non-zero and is the tool's OUTPUT — there is no flag that skips validation.

IT WRITES THE EPOCH OF ITS RELEASE: this binary writes event-schema epoch 38, the epoch of the feov-record it ships beside, and a migrated run is read with that feov-record. It is an operator's tool: it takes no seat id, and no seat's surface carries it.

THE PER-TURN MEASUREMENTS ARE BROUGHT ACROSS, not replayed: they are measurements taken from outside, from transcripts that are not part of a run, so the record holds the only copy of them and the manifest states how many came over. The manifest also states the epoch the source declared beside the one this binary wrote, so the move the migration exists to make is a fact you can read rather than infer.

THE REPORT'S TEXT IS BROUGHT INTO THE ORDER IT SHIPS IN (the title line (`# …`); `## TL;DR`; `## The Catechism`; the report's own sections, in the order their author chooses; `## Open questions`), where that moves whole sections and nothing else: the base is reordered and every act replayed over it, and the result is kept when no act is refused and the acts give the same text section for section. Otherwise the record is replayed as written and its text keeps its author's order. The manifest's report_order says which: no_report, in_order, reordered, as_written.

THE RUN IS BUILT BESIDE --to AND MOVED INTO PLACE WHEN IT IS COMPLETE. It is built in a directory named .<name>.migrating-<random> next to --to, its files are synced, and it is then moved onto --to, so an interrupted migration leaves --to as it was (on Windows, a --to that was an empty directory may be absent). Its directory entries are synced where the filesystem allows — best effort: a directory that could not be synced is a warning on stderr, and on Windows none are. A leftover .<name>.migrating-<random> directory beside --to is what an interrupted migration left behind; nothing reads it and nothing removes it, and it is safe to delete by hand when no migration is running.

--to must be absent, or an empty plain directory — not a link, a junction or a mount point — in a parent you can write, with a base name short enough to take the .migrating-<random> suffix. A separated record root (FEOV_RECORD_ROOT set) is refused. An existing empty --to is REPLACED, not filled: its mode, owner and attributes become the new directory's. On Unix a shell sitting in it is left in the old directory until it changes directory; on Windows a directory in use cannot be removed, so the move fails after the build and the built run is kept beside --to.

Usage:
  feov-migrate --from <oldRunDir> --to <newRunDir> [flags]

Flags:
      --accept-loss strings   accept a registry-documented loss for exactly this word (repeatable); the acceptance lands in the manifest
      --from string           the old run directory (its records/record.db file set is read from a copy)
  -h, --help                  help for feov-migrate
      --json                  print the manifest as JSON on stdout, and a refusal as the JSON refusal envelope, instead of text
      --to string             the run directory to publish — absent, or an empty plain directory (not a link, junction or mount point) in a writable parent; an empty one is replaced
  -v, --version               version for feov-migrate
